 It throws a `SyntaxError` when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer, and an `Error` when it does not
 resolve to a value of the document. Inherited object properties do not resolve.
+
+## JSON Patch
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies a JSON Patch ([RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)) to a
+JSON document and returns the resulting document. The patch is an array of the six operations `add`,
+`remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`; their paths are JSON Pointers. The input document and the patch
+are not modified.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from './src/index.ts';
+
+applyPatch({ foo: ['bar', 'baz'] }, [
+  { op: 'add', path: '/foo/1', value: 'qux' },
+  { op: 'test', path: '/foo/0', value: 'bar' },
+]); // { foo: ['bar', 'qux', 'baz'] }
+```
+
+It throws a `SyntaxError` when the patch or one of its operations is not valid, and an `Error` when an operation
+fails: its target does not exist, a `test` value differs, or a `move` would put a value inside its own child.
+No partial result is returned.
