+---
+id: task-002-json-patch-rfc-6902
+type: task
+title: "JSON Patch (RFC 6902)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches. Implement JSON Patch as specified in
+RFC 6902, with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`. Export a function
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a JSON Patch (an array of operations) to a JSON
+document and returns the resulting document as a new value. It must not mutate its input. It throws an error
+when the patch is invalid, or when one of its operations fails.
+
+It builds on `resolvePointer` (task-001, RFC 6901), which is already in `src/index.ts`.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Export `applyPatch(document: unknown, patch: unknown): unknown` from `src/index.ts`.
+- Doc comment on the exported function, stating the behaviour and the RFC it implements (directive:
+  documentation).
+- Tests under `test/` runnable with `npm test` (`node --test`).
+- No runtime dependency is added (README).
+- Shared pointer parsing is reused, not duplicated, by `resolvePointer` and `applyPatch`.
+
+## Acceptance criteria (RFC 6902)
+
+1. The patch must be an array of operation objects. Each operation has a string `op` that is one of `add`,
+   `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`, and a string `path`. `add`, `replace` and `test` require a
+   `value` member. `move` and `copy` require a string `from`. Otherwise the patch is invalid and throws.
+2. Operations apply in order. Each one acts on the result of the previous one. If any operation fails, the
+   whole call throws and nothing is returned.
+3. `add`: the parent of `path` must exist. For an object, the member is set (replacing it if present). For an
+   array, the token `-` appends; otherwise the index must be between `0` and the array length inclusive and
+   the value is inserted there. The path `""` replaces the whole document.
+4. `remove`: the target must exist. For an array the index must be less than its length; `-` does not resolve.
+   Removing the whole document (path `""`) is an error.
+5. `replace`: the target must exist. It replaces the value at `path`. Path `""` replaces the whole document.
+6. `move`: `from` must exist. It is removed from `from` and added at `path`, as `add` does. `from` must not be a
+   proper prefix of `path`: moving a value into its own child is an error. `from` equal to `path` is a no-op.
+7. `copy`: the value at `from` is copied (deep) to `path`, as `add` does.
+8. `test`: the value at `path` must exist and be deeply equal to `value` (object member order is ignored; array
+   order matters). Otherwise it throws.
+9. Values taken from the patch are copied, so later changes to the result do not alter the patch.
+10. The input document and the patch are not mutated; the result is a new value.
+11. Object members are set as own properties, so a key such as `__proto__` is handled as a plain key.
+12. Pointer errors (from RFC 6901 parsing) propagate as they do for `resolvePointer`.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396) and any other JSON Patch extension.
+- Streaming or incremental application.
